@startuml join-handshake
!theme plain
title Join handshake (revision 2): a server challenge, a signed canonical transcript, atomic invitation consumption; the out-of-band pin is the first anchor, DNSSEC/DANE a cross-check

participant "Donor bridge identity\n(curator remote-worker init)" as C
database "Trusted validating resolver\n(local, or validating stub over a protected channel)" as R
participant "Join point\n(our host)" as JP
actor "Operator" as OP
participant "Registry / key keeper" as T

note over C, T
  Anchors before first contact:
  (a) the invitation, delivered over a second channel: root fingerprint, SSH host key fingerprint, join point certificate, one-use random code (>= 128 bits);
  (b) when a trusted validator exists: DNSSEC-secure TXT _swarma.<domain> = root fingerprint, SSHFP = our SSH host key, TLSA = the join point's TLS key.
  Proof of possession: the transcript signed over a SERVER challenge (not a client nonce).
  Human check: the fingerprint read back over the second channel. No step downgrades on failure.
end note

C -> JP: POST /join/challenge {invitation_id}
JP -> JP: invitation known, unexpired, not consumed? attempt counter
JP --> C: server_challenge (5 min)
C -> R: TXT _swarma, SSHFP, TLSA
R --> C: records + validation state (secure | bogus | indeterminate)
C -> C: secure: must equal the invitation; bogus: refuse; indeterminate: proceed on the pin, record it
C -> C: transcript = canonical CBOR, domain-separated:\n{version, invitation_id, project, role, K_sig, K_enc, K_ssh, server_challenge, client_nonce, expires_at, algorithms}
C -> JP: TLS pinned to the join point certificate (invitation; TLSA when secure)
C -> JP: POST /join/cert {transcript, sig_K_sig(transcript), code}
JP -> JP: code equal (constant time)? signature valid? challenge bound and fresh?\nconsume the invitation atomically -> tombstone(invitation_id, K_sig)\n(same K_sig retry -> same pending enrolment; other key -> refuse); rate limit
JP -> OP: show {invitation_id, fingerprint, name, role}
OP -> OP: read the fingerprint back with the human on the donor side
OP -> T: pin K_sig, K_enc, K_ssh; root-signed admission; grants (scope, expiry)
OP -> OP: package: AEAD to K_enc with AAD = H(transcript); signed by the operator certificate (root-signed, scope onboard)
OP -> JP: release package for <invitation_id>
C -> JP: POST /join/challenge {invitation_id} (second challenge)
C -> JP: POST /join/collect {invitation_id, sig_K_sig(second challenge)}
JP --> C: package
C -> C: decrypt with K_enc; verify operator cert chain to the pinned root;\nverify package signature; check AAD; pin our SSH host key from the package\nAND cross-check against the invitation (and SSHFP when secure)

note over C, T
  Pinned afterwards:
  on our side K_sig, K_enc, K_ssh in the registry and K_ssh in the root-owned authorized-keys file of the workplace user;
  on the donor side our root, the operator certificate, our SSH host key, the binding digest, the qualified tuple, the policy generation.
  Every later message: signed over exact bytes by K_sig on the donor, fresh, replay-checked, grant-checked (ADM).
  Replay: cert/collect meet the tombstone; frames meet connection-scoped ids and op_key caches.
  Rotation: our host key -> new SSHFP + "init --rotate-host" with a fresh invitation; operator cert -> root-signed record; K -> revoke + new enrolment.
end note
@enduml
